00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.870
to talk about any, like,
totally exciting new features

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:09.490
because there are not any huge
new features in recent years

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:11.840
in, in the development
of knitr.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.290
But I would like to highlight
a few little things.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:18.760
So for a full list of
changes and new features,

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.490
you can check out the release
notes of knitr later.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:24.820
So the first thing
I want to highlight

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:29.300
is that now you have a new
way to write chunk options.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.500
Previously, you must
write all chunk options

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.460
on one line in the chunk
header, but now you

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:40.750
can write them on multiple
lines as special comments

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.480
in the beginning of
the chunk of body.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:48.100
So this special comment
start with the hash character

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.890
followed by the pipe character.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:55.690
I'm not sure how would you
pronounce this combination?

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:57.620
Maybe hash pipe?

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:02.590
I remember someone mentioned
me that hash pipe was

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.440
a funny coincidence.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.780
Since I'm not a native
English speaker,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.500
I had no idea what hash pipe
means, so I googled for that

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.790
and I regret it. Anyway,

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.170
hash pipe comments.

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:18.640
You can use that to
write chunk options.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.800
So as I said
previously, you have

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.700
to force all the chunk options
onto a single line, which

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.500
is sometimes very awkward.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:32.830
If you have many, many chunk
options for a single chunk,

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:36.800
there will be an awkward
horizontal scroll bar.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.720
But now you can write all of
your chunk options can write

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.490
With your chunk body.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:45.880
You can put them
inside the chunk

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.140
after the special comments.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.960
And you can also hard wrap
all these lines freely.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:57.380
You can write them on
any number of lines.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.420
So, for example, you can put
one chunk option on one line

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:06.100
or you can break a single
chunk option in the middle.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:08.650
You can wrap them
in any way you like,

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:14.560
and alternatively, you can also
use the YAML syntax to provide

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.150
your chunk options.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:19.420
So previously I'm
showing the old syntax

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:25.630
of comma separated chunk options
of the form option equals value

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.330
and separated by commas.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.860
If you're familiar
with yaml, that syntax

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.940
will be option colon
followed by value

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.870
and you have to write
one option per line

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.050
For Quarto in RStudio
or VS Code users.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:45.610
I strongly recommend that
you use the YAML syntax

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:49.900
because the editors,
RStudio and VS Code.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:54.100
They have better support
for the YAML options.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:59.110
For example, they can help you
out complete the option names.

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:03.380
They can validate the
values of your options.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.730
And second, the
YAML syntax works

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:11.210
for other engines in Quarto
as well, such as Jupyter.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:14.260
So if you want to
convert from a convert

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:17.650
the old syntax to the new
syntax, there is a function

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:20.800
and convert chunk
header in knitr.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.800
So you can use that
function to convert

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:29.290
your old Markdown documents to
using the new syntax for chunk

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:29.990
options.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:33.260
But this requires a very
recent version of knitr.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:37.090
And the second thing
I want to talk about

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:43.300
is how to reuse chunk, both
chunk options and chunk content

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.800
from other code chunks.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:48.910
So previously there
existed several ways

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:52.210
to reuse the content
of a code chunk,

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:57.190
for example, through
the ref.label option.

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:02.290
So suppose you have one code
chunk with the label chunk A

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:07.210
and then you can write another
code chunk with label chunk B,

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:13.120
but set the chunk option ref.label 
equals chunk A

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.500
ref.label equals chunk A

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:21.430
That means I want to copy
the chunk content of chunk

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:26.770
A into chunk B. So now although you
see the chunk B is empty here,

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:32.830
but when you execute this
document chunk B will

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:35.020
have the same
content as chunk A.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:39.100
You can learn more about
reusing the chunk content

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:41.140
in the R Markdown cookbook.

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:46.870
So now in addition to reusing
the content of code chunks,

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:51.940
you can also reuse chunk
options from other code chunks.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:57.040
And this can be done through
the chunk option ops dot label.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.110
So for example, you
have chunk A here

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.320
and you have set a couple
of chunk options on chunk A

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:07.000
and if you want to use the
same chunk options in chunk B,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:12.010
then you simply need, you
simply can set opts.label

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:13.400
equals chunk A.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:17.260
So instead of repeating all
the chunk options from chunk A,

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.500
you simply set a single option.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.510
opts.label equals
chunk A and then chunk B

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:26.480
will use the same chunk
options as chunk A.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:32.049
Then if you want to reuse both
chunk options and the content

00:08:32.049 --> 00:08:39.970
from other code chunks, you can
set the chunk option ref.label

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:43.720
to the label of
other code chunks

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:46.910
and then wrap the
labels in the function I.

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:52.210
For example, here I have chunk
A which has a few chunk options

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:55.700
and then it also has
some chunk content.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.840
And then in chunk B
I said ref.label equals

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:03.820
I, chunk A that means I want to
use both the content of chunk A

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:07.010
and the chunk
options of chunk A.

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:10.570
So in that way you don't have
to repeat everything from chunk A.

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:14.110
You simply set a single
option and knitr will

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:19.420
copy everything to the
next to chunk B

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:22.760
So I also want to
share two quick tips.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:27.400
First, the chunk options
ref.label and opts.label,

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:31.730
can take a
vector of chunk labels.

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:35.710
That means you can reuse
multiple code chunks instead

00:09:35.710 --> 00:09:38.650
of a single one. Second,

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:43.400
you can provide further
low code chunk options.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.620
If you, if a code chunk has
some local chunk options,

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:52.790
they will overwrite the reused
options of the same names.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:56.860
So in this example,
chunk B is basically

00:09:56.860 --> 00:10:01.840
reusing both chunk options and
the chunk content of chunk A,

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.290
but it has set a low code.

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:08.590
chunk option dev equals png.

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:11.410
So since we have
borrowed all the chunk

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:15.790
options from chunk A which
includes the chunk option

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:17.740
dev equals SVG.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:22.600
So now chunk B since chunk
B has a local chunk option

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:27.880
dev=png, this
equals png will override

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:31.180
the dev equals png from chunk a.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:37.210
So basically local chunk options
will have higher priorities

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:41.980
if you reuse chunk options
from other code chunks.

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:45.640
Third thing I want to mention
is some of the new chunk

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:46.740
options in knitr.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:50.630
In recent years we have
added a few of them.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:54.820
But today I'm just going
to mention one of them

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:57.730
here, which is the file option.

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:01.900
So sometimes you may
want to develop code

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:07.600
in an external script and
include it in code chunk.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:13.120
Previously what you could do is
to use the chunk option code.

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:15.490
So for example, here I'm
using the chunk option

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:21.550
code that takes the value
from red lines script.R

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:24.880
So that basically means I want
to read an external script

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:27.490
and pass the content
of that script

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:30.580
to the code option
and then knitr knows

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:33.460
I will use the
content of that script

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:35.520
as the content of this code.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:37.100
chunk.

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:41.920
That's the previous way of
including external scripts

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:43.930
into a code chunk.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:50.540
Now this can be simplified
to the chunk option file.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:51.980
So you can set file.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:55.000
You cause a file path,
for example, script.R.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.690
so basically that's
equivalent to using code

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.630
equals read lines.

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:04.060
Scripts are before and
this file option also

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:06.860
supports a vector of file paths.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:11.350
For example, you can pass two
scripts to the file option

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:15.010
and the file option
will read both of them

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:18.210
into the code chunk.

00:12:18.210 --> 00:12:21.540
The fourth thing I
want to highlight

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:24.930
is some new engines in knitr.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.190
The first one is comment engine.

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:28.710
So you can use
the comment engine

00:12:28.710 --> 00:12:32.160
to comment out any content.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:36.180
And the content can contain
basically anything, including

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:40.110
code chunks or
include or inline R

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:43.690
expressions or other
paragraphs or anything.

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:48.300
So basically there's one rule
that you have to remember.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:54.060
If the comment contains
three back ticks and then

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:58.600
for the comment engine, you
have to start with four backtics.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:02.160
So basically the rule is
that if your comment contains

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:05.310
any tactics, then
the comment engine

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:08.430
needs to start with
m plus 1 tactics.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.430
So, if the content
is commented out,

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:14.980
it will not be
included in the output.

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:18.760
And the second engine
is the verbatim engine.

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:21.300
And this can be very
useful when you want

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:25.080
to write tutorials on R Markdown.

00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:29.970
So and, and you may want to
display some content verbatim,

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:32.640
especially the code chunks.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:36.000
When you want to display
a code chunk verbatim,

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.540
you can use the verbatim engine
and the rule is the same.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:43.890
So for example, here I'm
including a code chunk

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:47.040
with three back tics and
then the verbatim engine

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:51.780
needs to start with four
back tics. For the verbatim engine,

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:56.580
depending on the
content of, depending

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:59.430
on the language of the
content, you can, you

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:04.200
can set a syntax highlighting
language through the chunk

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:05.080
option lang.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:07.990
So for example here I'm
using lang equals Markdown.

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:14.390
That means the verbatim
content here is Markdown code.

00:14:14.390 --> 00:14:18.950
But this lang
option is optional,

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:21.290
so it depends on
whether you want

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:24.020
to syntax highlighting
in your output

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:27.530
for the verbatim content.

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:33.290
Previously I have introduced
some old, ugly hacks

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:37.730
on how to show content
verbatim, and now you

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:40.430
can forget all of them.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:42.630
And here I just.

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:45.290
Yeah, I don't want to
explain this anymore.

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:47.450
It's just too ugly.

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:49.850
Use the verbatim
engine and forget

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:53.520
about the old, ugly hacks.

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:58.020
The third engine that I want
to show is the embed engine.

00:14:58.020 --> 00:14:59.640
So you can use this.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:04.890
You can use the embed engine
to embed external files,

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:05.860
text files.

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:09.180
Actually, you can embed
external text files

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.580
and display their
content verbatim.

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:15.510
So this is similar to
the verbatim engine,

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:22.380
but you can use the file
option to embed external files

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:27.040
so you don't have to write
the content in the chunk body.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:31.050
You just appoint the file
option to some file paths.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:33.690
And alternatively, you
can write file paths

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:37.510
in the chunk body
of the embed engine.

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:41.980
For example, you can write
quote, food.R, quote bar.R. .

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:47.680
Ah, so the second way
has a tiny advantage.

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:51.420
That is if you write
in the chunk body,

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:55.410
if you write to the file paths
in the chunk body, your editor,

00:15:55.410 --> 00:16:00.500
for example, RStudio may be able
to autocomplete the file paths.

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:06.690
The last engine that I want to
show here is the exec engine.

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:12.060
So this engine allows you to
execute an arbitrary system

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:17.490
command on a file that contains
the content of the chunk

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:22.710
so that command can be specified
through the chunk option

00:16:22.710 --> 00:16:23.620
command.

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:29.610
So, for example, I have
an exec engine here

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:32.700
and I'm using the
command equals bash.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:38.940
That means I want to run the
command bash on the command

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:41.270
that has specified.

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:43.400
In the chunk body.

00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:49.190
So basically when knitr
sees an exact chunk,

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:52.370
it will write the
chunk body into a file

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:56.450
and execute a
command on that file.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:16:59.030
So for this patch
example, actually, you

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:01.960
don't have to use
the exact engine.

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:05.569
You can simply use the
existing bash engine in knitr.

00:17:05.569 --> 00:17:10.609
So these two code chunks
are pretty much equivalent.

00:17:10.609 --> 00:17:15.020
There have existed a lot
of command based engines

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:20.119
in knitr, for example, arc
or go or Perl or Z Shell.

00:17:20.119 --> 00:17:22.400
And from now on,
we no longer need

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:26.750
to add a new engine for
every single command.

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:32.080
You can basically use the exact
engine to execute any command.

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:36.790
And by the way, the exact
engine is also extensible.

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:39.790
So you can, if you're interested
in go back and check out

00:17:39.790 --> 00:17:45.520
the examples, the example on
the link through the link here.

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:48.010
So for those who are
curious about how

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:53.530
the exact engine works,
so basically knitr

00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:59.350
will execute a command
of the form command args one,

00:17:59.350 --> 00:18:04.300
args two, sorry, command args one, args two

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:08.870
where
args is a file path.

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:11.290
And this file
contains the content 

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:14.500
of the code chunk and
args one and args

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:17.740
two are optional command
line arguments that you

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:21.750
may specify through the chunk
option engine.ops.

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:29.150
So here I have an example that
executes the grep command.

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:32.840
With a few command
line arguments I

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:35.870
and which means case
insensitive matching

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:42.530
and then the pattern is he
and I want to execute grep

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:48.080
on the four lines of
text here so he will

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:51.110
if we match each E in
case in sensitivity

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:54.470
it will match the first line
hellos in the third line

00:18:54.470 --> 00:18:57.590
since it has the
which contains H E.

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:02.160
And just a quick recap.

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:06.270
We have talked about four
things in today's talk.

00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:09.380
First thing is that you can use
hash pipe comments in the chunk

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:11.690
body to write chunk options.

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:15.170
Then you can use opts.label
and ref.label options

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:18.440
to re-use both chunk
options and content.

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:20.540
And third, you can
use the file option

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:24.440
to read external files
as the chunk content.

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:30.870
And last, we have introduced
a few new engines in knitr.

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:32.880
So that's all I
have for you today.

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:36.500
Thank you very much
and happy meeting.

